#Components

##FPGA — Lattice ECP5 `LFE5U-12F-6BG256C`
Main digital processing device.

Used for:
- capturing the 8-bit ADC output
- controlling acquisition
- FIFO buffering
- streamed readout
- JTAG/configuration support
### Why I chose it
The ECP5 provided significantly more digital capability than required for a basic 8-bit ADC interface while still being practical for a student designed PCB. 
It provides FPGA logic, block RAM,PLL/clocking resources and sufficient I/O for the parallel ADC interface.

For this project, a much larger FPGA such as an Artix-7 would have added cost and design 
complexity without providing a major advantage for an 8-bit, 32 MSPS-class acquisition system.

Supply rails:
- 1.1 V core
- 2.5 V auxiliary
- 3.3 V I/O

Local **100 nF** capacitors provide high-frequency decoupling, while 10 µF capacitors provide larger local energy storage.

##ADC — AD9280
8-bit ADC rated up to 32 MSPS.
Converts the analogue input into an 8-bit digital value from `0–255`, which is transferred in parallel to the FPGA.

##OPA836
### Why I chose it
A normal low frequency general-purpose op-amp would not be appropriate in front of a multi-megasample ADC because its bandwidth and settling performance could limit the signal before conversion.
The OPA836 was selected because it is a high speed amplifier intended for applications requiring substantially more bandwidth than general-purpose op-amps.

High-speed op-amp used to condition and buffer the analogue signal before it reaches the ADC.
A 49.9 Ω series resistor helps isolate the amplifier output from the ADC input and reduce ringing.

##25 MHz Oscillator
Provides the board clock.
Its output is routed to FPGA ball E7.
`E7` is the physical BGA pin through which the clock enters the FPGA.

##W25Q128JVS Configuration Flash
### Why I chose it
Unlike a microcontroller, the FPGA does not permanently retain its configuration after power is removed. External flash therefore allows the FPGA bitstream to be stored and loaded again during startup.

Stores the FPGA configuration so it can be loaded when the board powers up.

Powered from 3.3 V and locally decoupled using a **100 nF capacitor**.


##TLV62569 — 3.3 V Regulator
Buck regulator converting:

`5 V → 3.3 V`
The 3.3 V rail powers the FPGA I/O and digital circuitry.
A **4.7 µF input capacitor (C10)** is used near the regulator to reduce input ripple and provide local energy storage.


##TLV62569 — 1.1 V Regulator
Generates the FPGA core voltage:
5 V → 1.1 V

The FPGA core rail uses multiple:
- 100 nF capacitors** for high-frequency decoupling

- 10 µF capacitors** for bulk/local energy storage

##LT1963 — 2.5 V Regulator
Produces the FPGA auxiliary 2.5 V supply.
A linear regulator was used because the auxiliary rail benefits from a relatively clean supply and carries less current than the FPGA core rail.

##Ferrite Beads
Used to reduce high frequency noise between power domains.

Main uses:
- 3.3 V → ferrite bead → 3.3 VA
- filtering of the 2.5 V auxiliary supply

They pass DC power while presenting higher impedance to high frequency noise.

##Capacitor Strategy
Three main capacitor values are used:

Value - Purpose 
- 100 nF  - High-frequency IC decoupling 
- 4.7 µF  - Regulator input/local bulk capacitance 
- 10 µF  - Larger local energy storage 

The general idea is:
Power rail → bulk capacitor → local 100 nF capacitor → IC
The smaller capacitor handles fast switching noise, while the larger capacitor supports slower current changes.


##Grounding
A continuous internal ground plane is used rather than splitting analogue and digital ground.
This provides short return current paths and avoids forcing high-speed signals to cross gaps in the reference plane.
